When issuing a Digital Signature Certificate, a Certifying Authority shall certify that:
(a) it has complied with the provisions of this Act and the rules and regulations made thereunder [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 14, Section 36];
(b) it has published the Digital Signature Certificate or otherwise made it available to such person relying on it and the subscriber has accepted it [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 14, Section 36];
(c) the subscriber holds the private key corresponding to the public key, listed in the Digital Signature Certificate [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 14, Section 36];
(d) the subscriber's public key and private key constitute a functioning key pair [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 14, Section 36];
(e) the information contained in the Digital Signature Certificate is accurate [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 14, Section 36]; and
(f) it has no knowledge of any material fact, which if it had been included in the Digital Signature Certificate would adversely affect the reliability of the representations made in clauses (a) to (d) [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 14, Section 36].

Where the District Commission is satisfied that the goods complained against suffer from any of the defects specified in the complaint, it shall issue an order to the opposite party directing him to do one or more of the following:
(a) to remove the defect pointed out by the appropriate laboratory from the goods in question [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39];
(b) to replace the goods with new goods of similar description which shall be free from any defect [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39];
(c) to return to the complainant the price, or, as the case may be, the charges paid by the complainant along with such interest on such price or charges as may be decided [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39];
(d) to pay such amount as may be awarded by it as compensation to the consumer for any loss or injury suffered by the consumer due to the negligence of the opposite party [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39].

Where the District Commission is satisfied that the goods complained against suffer from any of the defects specified in the complaint, it shall issue an order to the opposite party directing him to do one or more of the following:
(a) to remove the defect pointed out by the appropriate laboratory from the goods in question;
(b) to replace the goods with new goods of similar description which shall be free from any defect;
(c) to return to the complainant the price, or, as the case may be, the charges paid by the complainant along with such interest on such price or charges as may be decided;
(d) to pay such amount as may be awarded by it as compensation to the consumer for any loss or injury suffered by the consumer due to the negligence of the opposite party.
[ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39]

A product manufacturer shall be liable in a product liability action if:
(a) the product contains a manufacturing defect; 
(b) the product is defective in design; 
(c) there is a deviation from manufacturing specifications; 
(d) the product does not conform to the express warranty; or 
(e) the product fails to contain adequate instructions of correct usage to prevent any harm or any warning regarding improper or incorrect usage.
[ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84]

Additionally, a product manufacturer shall be liable in a product liability action even if the manufacturer proves that they were not negligent or fraudulent in making the express warranty of the product [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84].

Based on the provided context, nothing in the Act applies to the following documents or transactions:

(a) a negotiable instrument as defined in section 13 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 2, Section 1];
(b) a power-of-attorney as defined in section 1A of the Powers-of-Attorney Act, 1882 [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 2, Section 1];
(c) a trust as defined in section 3 of the Indian Trusts Act, 1882 [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 2, Section 1];
(d) a will as defined in clause (h) of section 2 of the Indian Succession Act, 1925 including any other testamentary disposition by whatever name called [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 3, Section 1];
(e) any contract for the sale or conveyance of immovable property or any interest in such property [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 3, Section 1]; and
(f) any such class of documents or transactions as may be notified by the Central Government in the Official Gazette [ITAct2000.pdf, p. 3, Section 1].

When the District Commission is satisfied that the goods complained against suffer from any of the defects specified in the complaint, it shall issue an order to the opposite party directing them to do one or more of the following:

(a) to remove the defect pointed out by the appropriate laboratory from the goods in question [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39];
(b) to replace the goods with new goods of similar description which shall be free from any defect [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39];
(c) to return to the complainant the price, or, as the case may be, the charges paid by the complainant along with such interest on such price or charges as may be decided [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39];
(d) to pay such amount as may be awarded by it as compensation to the consumer for any loss or injury suffered by the consumer due to the negligence of the opposite party [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 22, Section 39].

A product manufacturer shall be liable in a product liability action if:
- the product contains a manufacturing defect [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84];
- the product is defective in design [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84];
- there is a deviation from manufacturing specifications [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84];
- the product does not conform to the express warranty [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84]; or
- the product fails to contain adequate instructions of correct usage to prevent any harm or any warning regarding improper or incorrect usage [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84].

Additionally, a product manufacturer is liable even if they prove that they were not negligent or fraudulent in making the express warranty of a product [ConsumerProtectionAct2019.pdf, p. 31, Section 84].
